## Key design points

1. **`hasMore` decides button visibility.** The button renders only when `hasMore` is true. The hook determines this in order of reliability:
   - an explicit `hasMore` flag from the API (best),
   - a `total` count compared to items loaded,
   - a fallback heuristic: if the page came back full, assume there may be more. This can cost one extra request that returns empty, after which the button disappears.

2. **Double-click protection.** A `ref` blocks concurrent requests, since state updates are asynchronous and `isLoading` alone can be stale during rapid clicks.

3. **Graceful states.** The component handles initial loading, the empty state, errors with a retry, and an end-of-list message.

4. **Accessibility.** The button is disabled with `aria-busy` while loading, and errors use `role="alert"`.

**Tip:** If your API uses cursor-based pagination (e.g. `{ items, nextCursor }`), store `nextCursor` instead of `page`, and set `hasMore = nextCursor != null`. That's the most reliable signal of all.

Here's a reusable, accessible React component that fetches items page by page and only shows the "Load more" button while more items remain.

```tsx
import { useCallback, useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

type Item = {
  id: number | string;
  title: string;
};

type PageResult<T> = {
  items: T[];
  hasMore: boolean;
};

type LoadMoreListProps<T> = {
  /** Fetches a page of items. Should resolve with the items and whether more exist. */
  fetchPage: (page: number, signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<PageResult<T>>;
  /** Renders a single item. */
  renderItem: (item: T) => React.ReactNode;
  /** Extracts a unique key for each item. */
  getKey: (item: T) => React.Key;
};

export function LoadMoreList<T>({ fetchPage, renderItem, getKey }: LoadMoreListProps<T>) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState<T[]>([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  // Guards against duplicate requests (e.g., double clicks) and stale updates.
  const inFlight = useRef(false);
  const abortRef = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

  const loadPage = useCallback(
    async (pageToLoad: number) => {
      if (inFlight.current) return;
      inFlight.current = true;
      setLoading(true);
      setError(null);

      const controller = new AbortController();
      abortRef.current = controller;

      try {
        const result = await fetchPage(pageToLoad, controller.signal);
        setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...result.items]);
        setHasMore(result.hasMore);
        setPage(pageToLoad + 1);
      } catch (err) {
        if ((err as Error).name !== "AbortError") {
          setError("Failed to load items. Please try again.");
        }
      } finally {
        inFlight.current = false;
        if (!controller.signal.aborted) setLoading(false);
      }
    },
    [fetchPage]
  );

  // Load the first page on mount; abort any pending request on unmount.
  useEffect(() => {
    loadPage(1);
    return () => abortRef.current?.abort();
  }, [loadPage]);

  return (
    <section>
      <ul aria-live="polite" aria-busy={loading}>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={getKey(item)}>{renderItem(item)}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {!loading && !error && items.length === 0 && !hasMore && <p>No items found.</p>}

      {error && (
        <p role="alert">
          {error}{" "}
          <button type="button" onClick={() => loadPage(page)}>
            Retry
          </button>
        </p>
      )}

      {/* Only render the button when more items exist and there's no error */}
      {hasMore && !error && (
        <button type="button" onClick={() => loadPage(page)} disabled={loading}>
          {loading ? "Loading…" : "Load more"}
        </button>
      )}

      {!hasMore && items.length > 0 && <p>You've reached the end.</p>}
    </section>
  );
}

/* ---------------- Example usage ---------------- */

const PAGE_SIZE = 10;

async function fetchPosts(page: number, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<PageResult<Item>> {
  const res = await fetch(
    `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?_page=${page}&_limit=${PAGE_SIZE}`,
    { signal }
  );
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

  const items: Item[] = await res.json();

  // Prefer a total count from the server when available...
  const total = Number(res.headers.get("x-total-count"));
  const hasMore = Number.isFinite(total) && total > 0
    ? page * PAGE_SIZE < total
    : items.length === PAGE_SIZE; // ...otherwise fall back to "full page = maybe more"

  return { items, hasMore };
}

export default function App() {
  return (
    <LoadMoreList
      fetchPage={fetchPosts}
      getKey={(post) => post.id}
      renderItem={(post) => post.title}
    />
  );
}
```

## How it works

**Deciding when to show the button**
- `hasMore` starts as `true` so the first fetch can happen.
- After each fetch, `hasMore` is updated from the API response. The button renders only when `hasMore && !error`.
- The `hasMore` logic lives in `fetchPage`, so the component works with any pagination style:
  - **Total count**: `page * pageSize < total`
  - **Cursor-based**: `Boolean(response.nextCursor)`
  - **No metadata**: `items.length === pageSize`. This is a heuristic. If the last page is exactly full, the user clicks once more and gets an empty page, which then hides the button.

**Robustness details**
- **No duplicate requests**: a `useRef` flag blocks concurrent fetches, even if the user clicks quickly before the disabled state renders.
- **Cleanup**: an `AbortController` cancels the pending request on unmount, avoiding state updates on an unmounted component.
- **Error handling**: on failure, the "Load more" button is replaced with a Retry button that reloads the same page.
- **Accessibility**: `aria-busy` and `aria-live` announce loading and new content to screen readers, and errors use `role="alert"`.

**Note:** pass a stable `fetchPage` (defined outside the component or wrapped in `useCallback`). Otherwise the effect will rerun on every render.

