## Key Design Decisions

| Concern | How it's handled |
|---|---|
| **When to hide the button** | `hasMore` is computed from `total` if available, otherwise from whether the last page was full. |
| **Double-click / race conditions** | A `useRef` flag blocks concurrent requests, and the button is disabled while loading. |
| **Errors** | The "Load more" button is replaced by a "Retry" button, so the user isn't stuck. |
| **Empty state** | "No items found" shows instead of a useless button. |
| **Accessibility** | `aria-busy` on the button and `role="alert"` on errors. |

**Note on the "full page" fallback:** if your API doesn't return a total and the item count is an exact multiple of `pageSize`, the user will click once more and get an empty page, after which the button hides. If your API supports a cursor (`nextCursor: null`) or a `hasNextPage` flag, use that instead. Just return it from `fetchPage` and set `hasMore` from it directly.

