## Using offset/total instead of cursors

If your API returns a `total` count rather than a cursor, compute `hasMore` like this:

```tsx
const PAGE_SIZE = 10;

async function fetchByOffset(cursor?: string): Promise<Page<Post>> {
  const offset = Number(cursor ?? 0);
  const res = await fetch(`/api/posts?offset=${offset}&limit=${PAGE_SIZE}`);
  const { items, total } = await res.json();
  const nextOffset = offset + items.length;
  return {
    items,
    nextCursor: nextOffset < total ? String(nextOffset) : null,
  };
}
```

If the API gives no total, a common heuristic is `items.length === PAGE_SIZE` means there may be more. It can cost one extra empty request at the end.

## Key points

- **The button only renders when `hasMore` is true.** `hasMore` is derived from the server's response (`nextCursor`), not guessed on the client.
- **Duplicate requests are blocked.** A `ref` guard stops rapid double-clicks from firing parallel fetches, and the button is disabled while loading.
- **Errors are handled.** The button stays visible and becomes "Retry", so a network blip doesn't hide the remaining items.
- **Accessibility:** `aria-live` announces new items, `aria-busy` reflects loading state, and errors use `role="alert"`.
- **Unmount safety:** state isn't updated after the component unmounts.

Here's a complete, reusable implementation with a paginated fetch, loading/error states, and a "Load more" button that only renders when more items exist.

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect, useCallback, useRef } from "react";

const PAGE_SIZE = 10;

// Example API call — replace with your real endpoint.
// Expected response shape: { items: [...], total: number }
async function fetchItems(page, pageSize, signal) {
  const res = await fetch(
    `/api/items?page=${page}&limit=${pageSize}`,
    { signal }
  );
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

export default function ItemList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(false);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const abortRef = useRef(null);

  const loadPage = useCallback(async (pageToLoad) => {
    abortRef.current?.abort();
    const controller = new AbortController();
    abortRef.current = controller;

    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    try {
      const { items: newItems, total } = await fetchItems(
        pageToLoad,
        PAGE_SIZE,
        controller.signal
      );

      setItems((prev) => {
        const merged = pageToLoad === 1 ? newItems : [...prev, ...newItems];
        // Determine if more items remain
        setHasMore(merged.length < total);
        return merged;
      });
      setPage(pageToLoad);
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.name !== "AbortError") setError(err.message);
    } finally {
      if (!controller.signal.aborted) setLoading(false);
    }
  }, []);

  // Initial load
  useEffect(() => {
    loadPage(1);
    return () => abortRef.current?.abort();
  }, [loadPage]);

  const handleLoadMore = () => {
    if (!loading && hasMore) loadPage(page + 1);
  };

  return (
    <div className="item-list">
      {items.length === 0 && !loading && !error && <p>No items found.</p>}

      <ul>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {error && (
        <div role="alert" className="error">
          <p>Something went wrong: {error}</p>
          <button onClick={() => loadPage(items.length ? page + 1 : 1)}>
            Retry
          </button>
        </div>
      )}

      {/* Only shown when there are more items to fetch */}
      {hasMore && !error && (
        <button
          onClick={handleLoadMore}
          disabled={loading}
          aria-busy={loading}
        >
          {loading ? "Loading…" : "Load more"}
        </button>
      )}

      {loading && items.length === 0 && <p>Loading…</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### How "has more" is determined

The key line is:

```js
setHasMore(merged.length < total);
```

If your API doesn't return a `total`, use one of these alternatives instead:

**1. Cursor / `hasMore` flag from the API (most robust):**
```js
const { items: newItems, nextCursor } = await fetchItems(cursor);
setHasMore(Boolean(nextCursor));
```

**2. Page-size heuristic (no metadata available):**
```js
setHasMore(newItems.length === PAGE_SIZE);
```
Note: this shows the button one extra time when the total is an exact multiple of the page size. The final click just returns zero items and hides it.

### Key details
- **Conditional rendering:** `{hasMore && ...}` removes the button entirely rather than just disabling it.
- **Double-click protection:** the button is disabled while loading, and `handleLoadMore` guards against concurrent requests.
- **Abort on unmount:** prevents state updates on an unmounted component and race conditions.
- **Error handling:** the button hides on error and a retry option appears instead.
- **Accessibility:** `aria-busy` signals loading state, and errors use `role="alert"`.

