## Notes on the design decisions

- **One source of truth.** `isOnSale` is computed once and drives the badge, the strikethrough price, and the discount percentage. No chance of a badge showing without a matching original price.
- **Defensive check.** `Number.isFinite(compareAtPrice) && compareAtPrice > price` handles `undefined`, `null`, `NaN`, and the "compare-at price accidentally set lower than the sale price" case.
- **Discount percent is optional.** If the math rounds to 0 (e.g. a 0.4% discount), the badge still shows "Sale" but skips the `−0%` suffix.
- **Accessibility.** The strikethrough is `aria-hidden` and replaced with a visually-hidden sentence, since screen readers don't announce `text-decoration: line-through`.
- **`position: relative` on the media wrapper** is what lets the badge sit in the corner of the image without affecting layout.

If your data shape uses different field names (e.g. `originalPrice`, `salePrice`, or a `discount` object), just swap the destructuring and the `isOnSale` expression — the rest of the component stays the same.

Here's a ready-to-use React product card component that displays a "Sale" badge only when the product is discounted, along with a clean and accessible design.
```jsx
import React from 'react';
import './ProductCard.css';

const currencyFormatter = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'USD',
});

export default function ProductCard({ product }) {
  const { name, image, price, originalPrice } = product;

  // Single source of truth for the "on sale" state.
  // Guards against undefined, null, 0, and non-numeric values.
  const isOnSale =
    Number.isFinite(originalPrice) &&
    Number.isFinite(price) &&
    originalPrice > price;

  const discountPercent = isOnSale
    ? Math.round(((originalPrice - price) / originalPrice) * 100)
    : 0;

  return (
    <article className="product-card">
      <div className="product-card__media">
        {isOnSale && <span className="product-card__badge">Sale</span>}
        <img
          className="product-card__image"
          src={image}
          alt={name}
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>

      <div className="product-card__body">
        <h3 className="product-card__name">{name}</h3>

        <div className="product-card__prices">
          <span className="product-card__price">
            {currencyFormatter.format(price)}
          </span>

          {isOnSale && (
            <>
              <s className="product-card__original-price">
                {currencyFormatter.format(originalPrice)}
              </s>
              <span className="product-card__discount">
                {discountPercent}% off
              </span>
            </>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  );
}
```
### Sale Badge Logic & Display

The card focuses on one clear job: showing a sale badge and adjusted pricing only when a product is actually discounted.

- **Discount detection** – The `isOnSale` flag checks that both `price` and `originalPrice` are finite numbers and that `originalPrice` is greater than `price`, so invalid or missing values never trigger the badge.
- **Conditional badge** – The "Sale" badge renders inside the media area only when `isOnSale` is true, keeping the UI clean for full-price items.
- **Price presentation** – The current price is always shown. When discounted, the original price appears with a strikethrough and a calculated percentage-off label.
- **Reusable formatting** – A shared `Intl.NumberFormat` instance formats all prices consistently as USD.
---

**Optimization Tip:** You can replace the `ProductCard.css` import and class names with your own styling solution, and adjust the `currency` option in `currencyFormatter` if your store uses a different currency.

