# Load More Button Component

Here's a React component that fetches paginated data and only renders the "Load more" button when more items are available:

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect, useCallback } from 'react';

const PAGE_SIZE = 10;

export default function ItemList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  const fetchItems = useCallback(async (pageNum) => {
    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    try {
      const res = await fetch(
        `/api/items?page=${pageNum}&limit=${PAGE_SIZE}`
      );
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${res.status}`);

      const data = await res.json();

      // Two common patterns for detecting "more":
      // 1) API returns an explicit flag
      // 2) Infer from batch size
      const moreAvailable =
        typeof data.hasMore === 'boolean'
          ? data.hasMore
          : data.items.length === PAGE_SIZE;

      setItems((prev) => (pageNum === 1 ? data.items : [...prev, ...data.items]));
      setHasMore(moreAvailable);
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  }, []);

  // Initial load
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchItems(1);
  }, [fetchItems]);

  const handleLoadMore = () => {
    const nextPage = page + 1;
    setPage(nextPage);
    fetchItems(nextPage);
  };

  return (
    <div className="item-list">
      <ul>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {error && <p className="error">Error: {error}</p>}

      {/* Only render the button when there is more data and we're not already loading */}
      {hasMore && !loading && (
        <button onClick={handleLoadMore}>Load more</button>
      )}

      {loading && <p>Loading…</p>}

      {!hasMore && items.length > 0 && <p>You've reached the end.</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Key points

1. **`hasMore` state** — the single source of truth for whether the button should appear. The button's visibility is gated by `hasMore && !loading`, so it never shows while a request is in-flight or after the last page.

2. **Two ways to determine `hasMore`:**
   - **Explicit flag** (preferred): API returns `{ items: [...], hasMore: boolean }`. Robust — works even when the last page happens to be full.
   - **Inferred**: If the batch size is less than `PAGE_SIZE`, there's no more data. Simpler but breaks if a page fills exactly and the next page is empty (you'd show the button one extra time).

3. **Race-condition safety** — `fetchItems` uses functional state updates (`setItems(prev => ...)`) so rapid clicks can't clobber earlier results. If you want to be extra safe against out-of-order responses, track an `AbortController` per request.

4. **Loading guard** — Disabling/hiding the button during `loading` prevents duplicate page fetches from double-clicks.

5. **First-page reset** — The `pageNum === 1` branch lets you reuse `fetchItems` for refresh/reset logic without concatenating.

## Optional: abortable version

If your users may navigate away mid-fetch, wrap the request:

```jsx
const controllerRef = useRef(null);

const fetchItems = useCallback(async (pageNum) => {
  controllerRef.current?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  controllerRef.current = controller;

  setLoading(true);
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/items?page=${pageNum}&limit=${PAGE_SIZE}`, {
      signal: controller.signal,
    });
    // ...
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setError(err.message);
  } finally {
    setLoading(false);
  }
}, []);
```

