      <ul>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {error && <p role="alert">Error: {error}</p>}

      {hasMore && (
        <button onClick={loadPage} disabled={loading}>
          {loading ? "Loading…" : "Load more"}
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Key idea

The button is rendered only when `hasMore` is `true`:

```jsx
{hasMore && <button onClick={loadPage} disabled={loading}>Load more</button>}
```

`hasMore` is derived from the server response (e.g. `data.hasMore`), so the button disappears automatically once the last page is reached.

### If your API doesn't return a `hasMore` flag

You can infer it from the number of items returned — a short page means you're at the end:

```jsx
setHasMore(newItems.length === PER_PAGE);
```

### Optional: Intersection Observer (infinite scroll)

Instead of a button, you can auto-load when the sentinel element scrolls into view:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!hasMore || loading) return;

  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    if (entries[0].isIntersecting) loadPage();
  });

  if (sentinelRef.current) observer.observe(sentinelRef.current);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, [hasMore, loading, loadPage]);
```

Just render `<div ref={sentinelRef} />` at the bottom of the list in place of the button.

