**Key points:**
- `hasMore` is the single source of truth for whether the button renders. It's set from the server response (e.g., `response.nextPage !== null`, `items.length < total`, etc.), so the button disappears automatically when the last page is reached.
- The `loading` state disables the button and shows a "Loading…" label to prevent double-clicks.
- The `cancelled` flag prevents state updates after unmount (avoids React warnings in StrictMode).
- `fetchPage` should be memoized (`useCallback`) in the parent, or moved outside the component, to avoid re-triggering the `useEffect` on every render.

**With a real API (e.g. JSONPlaceholder):**

```jsx
const fetchPage = useCallback(async (page) => {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/items?page=${page}&limit=10`);
  const data = await res.json();
  return {
    items: data.items,
    hasMore: data.page < data.totalPages,
  };
}, []);
```

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

const PAGE_SIZE = 10;

// Fake API: returns a slice of items and whether more exist.
async function fetchItems(offset) {
  const allItems = Array.from({ length: 42 }, (_, i) => ({
    id: i + 1,
    title: `Item ${i + 1}`,
  }));

  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 500)); // simulate network

  const items = allItems.slice(offset, offset + PAGE_SIZE);
  return {
    items,
    hasMore: offset + PAGE_SIZE < allItems.length,
  };
}

export default function ItemList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [offset, setOffset] = useState(0);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  const loadMore = async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    try {
      const { items: newItems, hasMore: more } = await fetchItems(offset);
      setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...newItems]);
      setOffset((prev) => prev + newItems.length);
      setHasMore(more);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  // Initial load
  useEffect(() => {
    loadMore();
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <ul>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>{item.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {loading && <p>Loading…</p>}

      {!loading && hasMore && (
        <button type="button" onClick={loadMore}>
          Load more
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

**How it works**

- `hasMore` tracks whether the server still has items left (in a real app this comes from the API response, e.g. `nextPage`/`hasMore`/`total`).
- The button renders only when `hasMore === true` **and** the component isn't currently loading, so it disappears once everything is fetched.
- `loading` is handled separately so the button can be replaced by a spinner during a request, avoiding duplicate clicks.

**With a real API (cursor-based)**

```jsx
const res = await fetch(`/api/items?cursor=${cursor}`);
const data = await res.json();
setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...data.items]);
setHasMore(Boolean(data.nextCursor)); // no cursor => no more items
setCursor(data.nextCursor);
```

**Tip:** If your API returns a `total` count, you can derive the condition directly instead of storing `hasMore`:

```jsx
const hasMore = items.length < total;
{hasMore && !loading && <button onClick={loadMore}>Load more</button>}
```

