**How the "only when there are more items" logic works:**

- `hasMore` state starts as `true` and is updated after each fetch by comparing how many items we've accumulated against the server-reported `total`.
- The button is rendered with `{hasMore && (...)}`, so it disappears entirely once all items are loaded.
- `loading` disables the button (and shows "Loading…") to prevent duplicate requests while a page is in flight.

**Variants depending on your API:**

```jsx
// If the API returns a `nextPage` / `nextCursor` field:
setHasMore(Boolean(data.nextPage));

// If the API returns a `hasMore` boolean directly:
setHasMore(data.hasMore);

// If you don't know the total and rely on a short page:
setHasMore(data.length === PAGE_SIZE);
```

**Note on the effect:** the `items.length + data.length` calculation uses the `items` value captured from the render that started the effect, which is correct here because `page` changes trigger a fresh render and a fresh closure each time. If you want to be extra safe with stale closures, you could use the functional updater form and compute `hasMore` inside it:

```jsx
setItems((prev) => {
  const next = [...prev, ...data];
  setHasMore(next.length < total);
  return next;
});
```

Here's a React component that displays a "Load more" button only when there are more items available to fetch:

```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

export default function ItemList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);

  const fetchItems = async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/items?page=${page}`);
      const data = await res.json();

      setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...data.items]);
      // Assume the API returns a `hasMore` flag
      setHasMore(data.hasMore);
      setPage((prev) => prev + 1);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error("Failed to load items", err);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <ul>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {hasMore && (
        <button onClick={fetchItems} disabled={loading}>
          {loading ? "Loading..." : "Load more"}
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Key points:**

- `hasMore` state controls visibility — the button is rendered only when `hasMore` is `true`.
- `loading` state disables the button and shows a "Loading..." label to prevent duplicate requests.
- The `hasMore` value comes from the API response (e.g., `data.hasMore`). If your API doesn't return this, you can infer it instead:

```jsx
// If the API returns a page of items and fewer than PAGE_SIZE come back,
// there are no more items.
setHasMore(data.items.length === PAGE_SIZE);
```

**Variant without an API flag** — track counts against a known total:

```jsx
const TOTAL_ITEMS = 100;
const PAGE_SIZE = 10;

// ...
setHasMore(items.length < TOTAL_ITEMS);
```

This way the button disappears automatically once all items have been loaded.
